# Three processes, because a turn is a job and a free-typed line is read by a
# model of its own. `bin/dev` runs them under foreman.
#
# `web` serves the pages and holds the Action Cable WebSocket. `jobs` is what
# actually plays a turn: NarrationJob classifies, moves, talks or narrates, and
# broadcasts the prose back over that WebSocket. Without `jobs` the browser
# accepts a command, echoes it back, and then nothing ever arrives -- the turn
# sits in the queue waiting for a worker.
#
# `classifier` is the classifier's local reader (`bin/pointer-classifier`): the
# small pointer model, which the engine asks at POINTER_CLASSIFIER_URL for
# every free-typed line. Without its weights it waits and says so, and
# free-typed lines are refused; it never stops the other two.
#
# No `-p` here on purpose: foreman sets `PORT` for every process it starts and
# `bin/rails server` honours it, so the port is `bin/dev`'s to choose -- which is
# what lets `PORT=3142 bin/dev` move the whole formation in one place. Pinning it
# here would silently win over that.
web: bin/rails server
jobs: bin/jobs
classifier: bin/pointer-classifier
